.TH MAC\-ANATOMY 1 "2026-05-21" "mac-anatomy 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mac\-anatomy \- map and audit the macOS root filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mac\-anatomy
[\fIGLOBAL\-FLAGS\fR]
[\fISUBCOMMAND\fR]
[\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Modern macOS (Big Sur and later) splits the boot volume into a sealed,
read-only System volume (the SSV) and a writable Data volume that is
firmlinked into the same namespace at
.IR /Users ,
.IR /Library ,
.IR /Applications ,
and so on. The result is a unified-looking root that is actually two
volumes glued together by per-path kernel redirections.
.PP
.B mac\-anatomy
maps that layout, audits it for surprising state, and explains
in-place why things are where they are. It is a read-only inspection
tool \- it never modifies the filesystem.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B overview
Quick summary: macOS version, boot volume mount status, SIP, root layout.
This is the default when no subcommand is given.
.TP
.B volumes
Parsed APFS container and volume structure via \fBdiskutil apfs list\fR.
Supports \fB\-\-json\fR.
.TP
.B firmlinks
List \fI/usr/share/firmlinks\fR with explanations of how each entry maps
a synthetic-root path to its actual location on the Data volume.
Supports \fB\-\-json\fR.
.TP
.B symlinks
Root-level symlinks (\fI/etc\fR, \fI/var\fR, \fI/tmp\fR, ...) resolved
with annotations explaining their NeXTSTEP/BSD origins.
Supports \fB\-\-json\fR.
.TP
.B sip
Current System Integrity Protection status plus a description of what
it protects (filesystem, NVRAM, kexts, debugging, task inspection).
.TP
.B setuid
Setuid and setgid binaries in
.IR /usr/bin ,
.IR /usr/sbin ,
.IR /bin ,
.IR /sbin ,
.IR /usr/libexec .
Surprises here are red flags. Supports \fB\-\-json\fR.
.TP
.BR world\-writable " | " ww
Find world-writable files in critical system directories (system
binaries, \fI/etc\fR, system frameworks, kernel extensions, launchd
agents and daemons). On a healthy system this returns zero hits.
Supports \fB\-\-json\fR.
.TP
.BR "tree " [ \fIdepth\fR ]
ASCII tree of the root volume to the given depth (default 2, max 6).
Uses
.B tree
from Homebrew if available; otherwise falls back to a find-based
indented listing.
.TP
.B doctor
Run a sequence of health checks against the root volume and print a
human-readable verdict. Exit codes:
.RS
.IP 0 4
healthy
.IP 1 4
warnings present
.IP 2 4
critical issue (SSV not sealed)
.RE
Supports \fB\-\-json\fR.
.TP
.BR "report " [ \fIpath\fR ]
Generate a single-file HTML report combining every section. Default
output path is \fI/tmp/mac-anatomy-\fIyyyymmdd\fI-\fIhhmmss\fI.html\fR.
.SH "GLOBAL FLAGS"
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output for subcommands that support it. Output is pure
JSON with no headers, banners, or ANSI escapes.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable ANSI color output even when stdout is a TTY.
.TP
.BR \-\-quiet ", " \-q
Suppress section headers in human-readable output.
.TP
.BR \-\-verbose ", " \-v
Extra detail (reserved for future use).
.TP
.B \-\-open
With
.B report
only: open the generated HTML in the default browser via
.BR open (1).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print usage and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Default overview:
.PP
.RS
.B mac\-anatomy
.RE
.PP
Generate a full HTML report and open it:
.PP
.RS
.B mac\-anatomy report \-\-open
.RE
.PP
Use the doctor exit code in a shell script:
.PP
.RS
\fBmac\-anatomy doctor || echo "investigate"\fR
.RE
.PP
Get machine-readable firmlinks for processing with jq:
.PP
.RS
\fBmac\-anatomy firmlinks \-\-json | jq '.[] | .path'\fR
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
Most subcommands exit 0 on success and non-zero on usage errors. The
.B doctor
subcommand uses a graded exit code as described above.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/firmlinks
Tab-separated list of synthetic-root → Data volume bindings consumed at
boot to set up firmlinks.
